The '''Industrialization and Upheaval period (1853–1875)''' covers the [[War of Liberation]] and its aftermath, the industrialisation of the Guajicutean economy, the guano boom on [[Warikú]], the influx of [[Kaftians|Kaftian]] refugees from [[Lacashe]], and the political realignment produced by ethnic violence and labour unrest. The period saw the transformation of [[Waribá]] into the island's principal industrial centre, the establishment of public universal primary education, and the emergence of a three-party political system in which a nativist labour party challenged the existing parties on anti-immigration grounds, producing a secret coalition government between the internationalist and rural-labour parties.

== War of Liberation (1853–1856) ==

The [[War of Liberation]] (1853–1856) was fought by a coalition including [[Burawa]], [[Volisania]], [[Balisca]], and [[Asikyira]] against [[Lacashe]], the last slave-holding state in the region. Guajicutea participated with a small troop assignment and granted docking rights to Burawa and Volisania, allowing their fleets to stage from [[Waribá]] and [[Barikú]]. The war ended with the [[Treaty of Guledga]] (1856), which abolished slavery in Lacashe and partitioned the nation: the eastern coastal strip became the [[Free Republic of Libertalia]], a state for freed slaves and freedmen, while the western and interior territories continued as the Republic of Lacashe.

The post-war period in Lacashe, known as the Bloody Decade (1856–1866), was marked by lynchings, population transfers, and communal violence. The disorder in Lacashe produced a wave of Kaftian refugees who crossed the sea to Guajicutea.

== International context ==

=== Balboa independence ===

[[Balboa]], a Volisanian viceroyalty, gained independence from [[Volisania]] in 1854. The independence of Balboa reflected a broader restructuring of Volisanian colonial holdings and contributed to the realignment of Illpynian powers during this period.

=== Three threats of Fosia ===

The term "[[Three threats of Fosia]]", coined by Volisanian politician Ernesto Bonastre in 1878, referred to the nations of [[Mitoyagi]], [[Zong]], and [[Nakamizu]] — the core industrial and military powers of the Fosian continent. Between 1850 and 1895, these nations experienced the strongest period of industrial, economic, and military growth in Adonia. The Illpynian powers, including Volisania, Burawa, Mercia, and Daras, grew increasingly alarmed by the accumulation of Fosian power and its potential threat to Illpynian allied nations in the New World. This realignment brought the Illpynian powers closer together and reshaped their colonial and diplomatic policies, with consequences for smaller states in the Capuyaquiran Sea.

=== Renewed Volisanian influence ===

Following the War of Liberation, in which Volisania had been a coalition partner, Volisanian influence in Guajicutea increased through investment and trade. Volisanian commercial interests invested in Guajicutea's economy, particularly in port infrastructure and manufacturing. This marked a shift from the Sartan-dominated commercial influence of the previous decades, as Volisania became a significant economic partner alongside [[Sarta]].

== Industrialisation ==

The mechanisation of farms in the 1850s and 1860s reduced agricultural labour requirements and prompted a migration from rural areas to cities. Sartan investment helped establish the first ironworks in [[Waribá]] in 1864, marking the beginning of heavy industry in Guajicutea. The ironworks, combined with the existing [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] and the port, transformed Waribá into the island's principal industrial centre. The city grew rapidly, overtaking [[Tabakurí]] as the most populous city on the island.

== Guano boom ==

The guano boom, beginning in 1853, created a new export industry centred on [[Warikú]], a small island near Guajicutea that had joined the Republic in 1756. Warikú possessed large guano deposits that became valuable as fertiliser in the mid-19th century. A sub-group of the Guaji population inhabited Warikú and had maintained autonomy since the island's accession to the Republic. The economic value of the guano deposits created tension between the autonomous inhabitants and the central government and commercial interests over guano extraction rights and revenues.

== Kaftian immigration and ethnic violence ==

The post-war disorder in Lacashe produced an influx of Kaftian refugees who crossed the sea to Guajicutea, arriving primarily at Waribá and Barikú. The Kaftian population nearly doubled, from approximately 17,000 in 1853 to approximately 36,000 by 1875. Both the [[Partido del Internacionalismo|PdI]] and the [[Partido por la Tierra|PpLT]] expressed sympathy for the refugees, but the influx produced significant resentment among the native population.

Anti-Kaftian sentiment manifested in attacks on Kaftian homes and businesses. The state attempted to maintain peace through military occupations deployed to protect Kaftian immigrants, but these interventions frequently resulted in clashes between soldiers and local populations. A race riot in 1866 marked a major eruption of ethnic violence. In the aftermath, a ban on immigration from all of [[Abyala]] was established, reducing new immigration but not addressing the discrimination faced by the existing Kaftian population.

== Workers movements ==

The industrialisation of the 1860s produced the first organised workers movements in Guajicutea. The first workers riot occurred in 1861, triggered by the use of Kaftian workers for low wages by employers seeking to undercut native labour. The riot reflected the tension between native workers and immigrant workers, with employers exploiting cheaper Kaftian labour. The riot directly led to the formation of the [[Partido de Estabilización]] (PpE).

== Political development ==

=== Three-party system ===

The political landscape of the period was defined by three parties. The [[Partido del Internacionalismo]] (PdI), established in 1832, supported free trade and international integration. The [[Partido por la Tierra]] (PpLT), established in 1851, advocated for rural and worker interests. The [[Partido de Estabilización]] (PpE), formed in 1861 after the first workers riot, was a nativist labour party: protoleftist in its economic platform, protectionist in its trade policy, and opposed to immigration.

The PpE's appeal grew with anti-Kaftian sentiment, intensifying after the 1866 race riot. The party overlapped with the PpLT on labour issues but diverged on immigration, and with the PdA on protectionism while being more economically left-wing.

=== PdI-PpLT secret coalition (1872) ===

In the 1872 [[Asamblea Nacional]] election, the PpE won a plurality of seats but not an outright majority. The PdI and PpLT formed a backroom agreement establishing a secret coalition government, combining their seats to form a majority. The coalition was a marriage of convenience: the PdI feared the PpE's protectionism would undermine trade liberalisation, while the PpLT feared the PpE's anti-immigration stance would divide the working class. The coalition allowed the PdI to pursue trade policy while giving the PpLT influence over labour and education legislation.

== Public universal primary education ==

Public universal primary education was established in 1870 under President [[José Moku]]. The state had been building parallel secular institutions since the 1824 church-state separation, with civil schools operating alongside parish schools. Universal primary education extended this system to all children, state-funded. The legislation was driven by the demands of industrialisation for literate workers, the political pressure of workers movements, and competition between parties for working-class support. The PdI signed the act with PpLT support in the Asamblea, an early demonstration of the cooperation that would later formalise in the 1872 coalition.

== Demographics ==

The population of Guajicutea grew from approximately 195,000 in 1853 to approximately 250,000 by 1875. The growth rate of approximately 1.2% per year was faster than the previous era, driven by natural increase and Kaftian immigration.

