|-
| Sartan / Sartan-Guaji mixed || ~28,000 || 14% || ~31,000 || 12% || Lower birth rates. Some Volisanian arrivals.
|-
| Kaftian || ~17,000 || 9% || ~36,000 || 14% || ~15k refugees (1856–1866) + natural increase. Nearly doubled.
|-
| Other || ~4,000 || 2% || ~8,000 || 3% || Growing — Volisanian traders, Zong merchants.
|-
| '''Total''' || '''~195,000''' || || '''~250,000''' || ||
|}

The Kaftian share of the population nearly doubled from 9% to 14%, the demographic shift that drove the ethnic violence and political realignment of the period.

=== Urbanisation ===

Urbanisation accelerated during this period, driven by industrialisation and the guano boom:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Year !! Urban !! % !! Rural !! %
|-
| 1853 || ~64,000 || 33% || ~131,000 || 67%
|-
| 1875 || ~107,000 || 43% || ~143,000 || 57%
|}

[[Waribá]] overtook [[Tabakurí]] as the largest city, reaching approximately 45,000 residents by 1875, driven by the ironworks, port activity, and manufacturing. Tabakurí grew more slowly to approximately 30,000, while Barikú reached approximately 18,000.

== Libertad-Lacashe War (1875–1880) ==

In 1875, [[Lacashe]] launched an invasion of [[Libertalia]], initiating the [[Libertad-Lacashe War]]. The war, which lasted five years and was fought primarily on Libertalian territory, extended beyond the end of this period. [[Asikyira]] deployed a substantial army that eventually pushed Lacashian forces back across the border. The war ended in 1880 with a peace treaty reimposing the partition and guaranteeing Libertalia's independence.

